{"status": "FAILED", "backend_state": "anthropic-tool-use", "degraded": false, "objective": "How does PostgreSQL's MVCC implementation provide transaction isolation, and why does it cause table bloat that VACUUM must clean up?", "run_id": "run_2c16c52fd0ef4554", "uncertainty": "UNCERTAIN", "termination_status": "FAILED", "unmet_conditions": [], "digest": {"objective": "How does PostgreSQL's MVCC implementation provide transaction isolation, and why does it cause table bloat that VACUUM must clean up?", "iteration": 2, "termination_status": "FAILED", "uncertainty": "UNCERTAIN", "subquestions": [], "hypotheses": [], "claims": [{"id": "clm_d873b02e18084b2d", "text": "The SQL standard defines four levels of transaction isolation.", "status": "CERTIFIED", "uncertainty": "EVIDENCE_FOR"}, {"id": "clm_e4add70228834114", "text": "The SQL standard and PostgreSQL-implemented transaction isolation levels are described in Table 13.1.", "status": "CERTIFIED", "uncertainty": "EVIDENCE_FOR"}, {"id": "clm_6c4745edb5c846c4", "text": "This is because it is the only sensible way to map the standard isolation levels to PostgreSQL's multiversion concurrency control architecture.", "status": "CERTIFIED", "uncertainty": "EVIDENCE_FOR"}, {"id": "clm_59fed7ec81434633", "text": "The table also shows that PostgreSQL's Repeatable Read implementation does not allow phantom reads.", "status": "CERTIFIED", "uncertainty": "EVIDENCE_FOR"}, {"id": "clm_1ae1534c8d01407e", "text": "To set the transaction isolation level of a transaction, use the command SET TRANSACTION.", "status": "PENDING", "uncertainty": "UNCERTAIN"}, {"id": "clm_0326909ec3c448c1", "text": "The partial transaction isolation provided by Read Committed mode is adequate for many applications, and this mode is fast and simple to use; however, it is not", "status": "PENDING", "uncertainty": "UNCERTAIN"}], "evidence_summary": {"total": 32, "relevant": 5, "supports": 4, "refutes": 0, "qualifies": 1, "circular_rejected": 0}, "sources": {"to
